e country that's  gonna stand up and say we want to be a  punitive thing we don't care about  science despite the fact that every  meta-analysis of scientific literature  will show you that punishment does not  equal a deterrent  we are still so hell-bent on being a  violent society that wants to do even  thinks that these things would work like  we're going down a pathway that is  incredibly concerning to me and  obviously it is to you  you use the word systemic a little bit  earlier in the interview and I think if  we sort of imagine a spectrum you might  have those completely on one side who  believe that the issues with police  violence and police brutality are 100%  systemic and that there's very little  blame to place on the individual  officers on the other side of that  spectrum you might imagine those who  believe this is merely a few bad apples  only the individual police officers are  to blame here and there is nothing  systemic going on the truth is probably  sort of somewhere in the middle of that  but I want to ask you where do you think  it is in other words how much of this  problem is the individuals how much of  the problem is systemic  I mean I'm thrown out of [ __ ] Park  number but I'm gonna go with probably 95  to 99 percent is completely systemic the  the way I can kind of encapsulate this  on an individual intimate level here is  that I grew up trying to be race blind I  mean I grew up in the age of information  in mixed neighborhoods and I had this  idea to myself that as a police officer  what I was going to do is be impartial I  was gonna follow the letter of the law I  was gonna follow a departmental policies  to a tee and it man it took me a while  but I've realized eventually at the end  that all of my actions of what I was  empirically doing on the street was was  racist  it was Presidents against a society it  was inhumane it was it was in just and  all of those things that I was doing I  was really looking at the system and  saying if we do this perfectly if we do  this system perfectly then we will have  justice so explain that a little bit  like for example I mean know having  interviewed you before would one example  of what you're talking about be despite  the reality that you went  wanting to be race blind and not  discriminating etc for example by virtue  of the neighborhoods you were policing  the impact of your policing was going to  be effectively racist is that an example  of what you mean yeah that's a good  example or whether I put someone into  bonds or whether I was looking at so I  thought I was a narcotics detective  hunting down narcotics but I wasn't  there was an entire half of that city  which we know empirically without a  doubt then anyone can argue it and I  know it from practical experience the  white communities have just as much  drugs if not more one reason why cocaine  sentencing is much higher is not because  of a racist thing intentionally it's  because cocaine is a white drug and so  white drugs end up having more resources  so a mid-level dealer ends up being much  higher on the scale of being a resource  same damn word I used a second ago but  so those those things that we end up  doing we think this is normal but when  we end up going after the drug did where  do they put me they put me in East and  West Baltimore to go after drug dealers  so I end up going after only the  community because the drug dealing and  drug possession is actually equal if not  more in a rich and white community think  about it drugs are expensive  I mean people with money have more drugs  than people without money this is even  rational but where was I sent to go get  the drugs in redlined  poor neighborhoods to put into an  inhumane system that how's about a  system that they can't / can't do so  it's not like it's not necessarily the  officers fault that when they're doing  that and even if they do it well they  send people into a system so even if you  had a police department that 